  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe current approach uses multiple optimization phases to find a minimizer of the overlap integral. However, the search space and optimization parameters might not be fully leveraging the structure of the problem. We introduce a hybrid physics-inspired optimization with wavelet-based initialization to better capture periodic patterns. We also implement a hierarchical basin hopping strategy with adaptive temperature decay and improved correlation function computation to enhance exploration.\n\nMathematical specification:\n- Represent h(x) as a piecewise constant function on a grid\n- Objective: minimize C5 = max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx over [0,2]\n- Constraint: \u222b h(x)dx = 1\n- For grid optimization, use mode='valid' for correlation to remove ends\n- Maintain valid solutions by projection onto the feasible set\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Parent validation and initialization:\n   - Check if initial_h_values provided\n   - Validate solution against constraints\n   - Project to feasible set if needed\n\n2. Physics-inspired phase:\n   - Generate wavelet-like patterns for initial candidates\n   - Implement scaled Stochastic Process Embedding\n   - Use adaptive momentum direction\n\n3. Hierarchical basin hopping:\n   - Progressive resolution refinement starting from coarsest grid\n   - Three levels: coarse, medium, fine resolution\n   - Each level uses SLSQP with summing and repair\n\n4. Main optimization loop:\n   - Temperature schedule: 1000 * (1 - iteration/3000)\n   - Scanning descent via autocorrelation peaks\n   - Correlation evaluation with mode='valid'\n\n5. Solution storage and acceptance:\n   - Track best solution by C5 value\n   - Maintain temperature and grid size schedules\n\n6. Stop criteria:\n   - Time budget exhausted\n   - 500 consecutive worse solutions\n   - Null improvements below current threshold\n\nSearch choices:\n- Initial wavelets use Daubechies coefficients\n- Adaptive temperature decay during hopping\n- Variable grid resolutions: starting from 0.1 resolving power\n- SLSQP maximizes autocorrelation while maintaining sum constraint\n\nCompute budget:\n- Coarse resolution: 1000 points, 100 seconds\n- Medium resolution: 2000 points, 500 seconds\n- Fine resolution: 5000 points, 3600 seconds\n\nValidation:\n- Compute correlation with mode='valid'\n- Ensure sum(h) = n_points/2\n- Clip and repair to [0,1] for all x\n\nTemperature schedule and hyperparameters:\n- Start temperature: 2000.0\n- Temperature decay rate: 0.998\n- Search window for window hopping: [0.1, 0.5, 1.0]\n- Minimum improvement: 1e-5",
